questions.question:
Analyze the following C# Singleton implementation. Why is it <b>not thread-safe</b>?<br><pre><code>public class Settings {
  private static Settings _instance;
  private Settings() {}
  public static Settings GetInstance() {
    if (_instance == null) {
      _instance = new Settings();
    }
    return _instance;
  }
}</code></pre>

Analyze the following thread-safe implementation. What is the role of the <b>volatile</b> keyword?<br><pre><code>public class DBConn {
  private static volatile DBConn _instance;
  private static readonly object _lock = new object();
  public static DBConn GetInstance() {
    if (_instance == null) {
      lock (_lock) {
        if (_instance == null)
          _instance = new DBConn();
      }
    }
    return _instance;
  }
}</code></pre>

